#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ea153ff-ca9a-53b6-87e8-2bc6a0c2501b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372099} {:answer/id #uuid "84df548c-6bde-5a63-97d5-4fbf449c3966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372100} {:answer/id #uuid "a3202131-59f4-5fd9-9f90-b0703666599e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372101} {:answer/id #uuid "dfaf9c30-a918-58ab-9ce8-23b59599fcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372102} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2c2c57-5714-5fbf-9c4e-fdaf923dacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f2c2c57-5714-5fbf-9c4e-fdaf923dacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372103} :answer-field/id #uuid "b38ccff0-d5a9-53de-b4dc-3a512c813886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe6d87cd-8e17-54ec-91d3-1c49d7e4c19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162545" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1cac5607abe416bf2ba5b4a6af42315edcc898034331034de68dd4c9be151c5e.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,ds$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,ds={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,ds.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,ds=0.\n$$"}]}
